## Performance Analysis — `StringReadability.cs`

**Context:** Hot path — every user-facing string in a CMS with hundreds of dynamic strings.

---

### Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.ToLower()`/`.ToUpper()` | 2 (L97, L103) |
| `params` allocation | 1 (L213) |
| LINQ `.Select`/`.All`/`.Cast` | 5 (L88, L89, L93, L101, L171) |
| `.Replace(` | 1 (L187) |
| `.Split(` | 2 (L75, L158) |
| Unsealed classes | 3 (L226, L232, L237) |
| `result[1..]` substring alloc | 1 (L106) |
| `GeneratedRegex` (positive) | 2 ✅ |

---

### 🔴 Critical

#### 1. Recursive `Humanize()` → `Pascalize()` infinite-loop risk & compound allocation storm (L171, L187)

**Impact:** `Dehumanize` calls `word.Humanize().Pascalize()` per word; `Pascalize` itself calls `input.Humanize().Transform(...).Replace(" ", "")`. Each `Dehumanize` call on N words does **3N+ allocations** (Split array + N×Humanize + N×Transform + N×Replace + final Concat). For a 4-word string that's ~16 allocations per call.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L171`, `L187`

**Fix:** `Pascalize` should capitalize the first char and lowercase the rest directly without re-humanizing. `Dehumanize` should use a `StringBuilder` or `string.Create` instead of LINQ+Concat.

```csharp
public static string Pascalize(this string input)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input))
        return input;
    // Capitalize first letter of each word, remove spaces — no re-Humanize
    Span<char> buffer = stackalloc char[input.Length];
    int pos = 0;
    bool capitalizeNext = true;
    foreach (char c in input)
    {
        if (c == ' ' || c == '_' || c == '-') { capitalizeNext = true; continue; }
        buffer[pos++] = capitalizeNext ? char.ToUpperInvariant(c) : char.ToLowerInvariant(c);
        capitalizeNext = false;
    }
    return new string(buffer[..pos]);
}
```

---

### 🟡 Moderate

#### 2. LINQ chain on regex matches — allocates enumerator, delegate, and intermediate collections (5 sites)

**Impact:** `FromPascalCase` uses `.Cast<Match>().Select(...).All(...)` which allocates 3+ enumerators and a delegate closure per call. On hundreds of strings this is significant GC pressure.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L88-89, L93, L101, L171`

**Fix:** Replace with a `foreach` over `MatchCollection` (which is directly enumerable in .NET 7+) and build result with `ValueStringBuilder` or `Span<char>`.

```csharp
internal static string FromPascalCase(string input)
{
    var matches = PascalCaseWordPartsRegex().Matches(input);
    if (matches.Count == 0) return input;

    var sb = new ValueStringBuilder(stackalloc char[128]);
    foreach (Match match in matches)
    {
        if (sb.Length > 0) sb.Append(' ');
        var value = match.ValueSpan;
        bool allUpper = true;
        foreach (char c in value) { if (!char.IsUpper(c)) { allUpper = false; break; } }

        if (allUpper && (value.Length > 1 || (match.Index > 0 && input[match.Index - 1] == ' ') || value is "I"))
            sb.Append(value);
        else
        {
